# deploy/docker/.env.example — copy to .env beside compose.yaml and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && $EDITOR .env
#
# .env is git-ignored and holds this node's secrets. It is the only file that
# differs between nodes; everything else comes from the image. Nothing here is
# ever committed — if a token ends up in a commit, rotate it rather than
# rewriting history.
#
# .env itself must be `chmod 600` — the same protection the Approver App's
# private key below already gets. A world- or group-readable .env hands every
# token in it to any other account on the host; check-node-compose.sh flags a
# .env that is not 0600. Rotate a token by editing .env in place, or via a
# 0600 temp file swapped into place — never leave a dated copy (.env.bak-*,
# *.env.old) beside the live one; check-node-compose.sh flags those too.

# --- Identity -----------------------------------------------------------
# The node's name, used as its Tailscale hostname and stamped into its logs.
# Give each node a name you would recognise in a list at 3am.
NODE_NAME=

# active | standby. An active node runs cycles and opens pull requests; a
# standby keeps its schedule but stands each cycle down. Any number of nodes
# may be active at once — per-item claims keep them off each other's work, so
# the role elects no leader; it just says whether this node spends (see
# AGENT_OPS_ROLE in the specs). Anything that is not exactly "active" means
# standby, including a typo.
ROLE=standby

# --- Which services this node runs ---------------------------------------
# tailnet | local, plus auto-update. See the header of compose.yaml.
# Use "local,auto-update" on a machine with no tailnet or no authkey.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=tailnet,auto-update

# --- GitHub --------------------------------------------------------------
# A token with the scopes the cycles need: read and write on the target
# repositories (contents, pull requests, issues), read on their alerts, and
# read/write on the private state repository named by `state_repo` in
# config.json — every node publishes its own state branch there and reads its
# peers' branches to stay warm.
# Prefer a fine-grained token, scoped to the Poetic-Poems repositories, with an
# expiry — and a separate one per node, so a single node can be revoked. This
# is what every cycle authenticates with by default, and the only credential
# needed to bring a node up at all; the forge authoring App below (D18
# decision 1) is the fleet's preferred long-term identity once provisioned,
# but this token is what a node degrades to — always, silently — whenever
# that App is unset, unreadable or unreachable, so it is never optional in
# practice while a node's own App key is not yet in place.
GH_TOKEN=

# The identity the agents' commits carry on every pull request this node
# opens. Required — an active node's cycles refuse to run, rather than commit
# under any default, if either is unset.
GIT_USER_NAME=
GIT_USER_EMAIL=

# --- Model credentials (D4) ------------------------------------------------
# Every stage runs through `claude`, which needs exactly one of the two paths
# below. Leave both empty and the node cannot run a cycle at all —
# `scripts/doctor.sh` and the container's own entrypoint both say so.
#
# BYO API key — the primary, first-class path. Set this and there is nothing
# else to do: `claude` reads it directly on every invocation, non-interactively,
# and any number of nodes may share the same key.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Claude subscription OAuth — a supported self-hosted alternative, not the
# primary path, and not set here at all: there is no env var for it. Leave
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY above empty and instead authenticate this node
# interactively, once, after `docker compose up -d`:
#
#   docker compose exec scheduler claude
#
# The credentials land in the claude-config volume (below), which is why that
# volume — alone among this stack's volumes — cannot be rebuilt from the
# image. Two constraints come with this path: the login is interactive per
# node, so it does not scale past a handful of nodes the way the API-key path
# does; and the subscription's terms limit it to your own use, not a service
# you operate for others.

# --- Egress fence (D24) ---------------------------------------------------
# The scheduler reaches the internet only through the egress-proxy service's
# domain allowlist (deploy/docker/egress-allowlist.txt ships the fleet-wide
# list). This variable adds this node's own extra domains — comma- or
# whitespace-separated — without touching the shipped list. The expected
# case is a Vercel project serving previews from a custom domain; most nodes
# leave it empty. There is no variable that turns the fence off: that is a
# compose.yaml edit, made deliberately or not at all.
EGRESS_EXTRA_ALLOW=

# --- Pullwright Approver App (D18, optional) ------------------------------
# The non-author GitHub App identity that reviews pull requests at every
# `merge_autonomy` level above `human` (D18 §5.3, lib/approver-token.sh).
# GitHub refuses self-approval, and this pipeline authors as its own configured
# owner, so an App is what makes a required approval load-bearing rather than
# theatre.
#
# Leave all three empty and every cycle runs exactly as it does at `human`:
# `approver_token_get` reports the gate unreadable, the Approver stage skips
# the App review rather than blocking the pull request, and the human still
# merges. But an operator who raised `merge_autonomy` and left these unset is
# waiting on approvals that will never come, so `scripts/doctor.sh` warns on
# exactly that pairing — check its output after setting these.
#
# The App id is not a secret (it is also `approver_app_id` in config.json, and
# doctor.sh fails a mismatch between the two). The installation id is the
# installation on *this* organisation: Settings → GitHub Apps → the App →
# Install App, or read it from `/orgs/<org>/installations`.
PULLWRIGHT_APPROVER_APP_ID=
PULLWRIGHT_APPROVER_INSTALLATION_ID=

# An absolute path **on the host** to the App's `.pem` private key. compose
# bind-mounts it read-only at this same path inside the container, so one value
# is correct on both sides and nodes that keep their key in different places
# need no other change. The file must be readable by uid 1000 (the container's
# `agent` user); `chmod 600` owned by a uid-1000 host user is the intended
# shape. Keep it out of every repository — it is the one credential here that
# cannot be rotated by editing a token field.
PULLWRIGHT_APPROVER_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=

# --- Forge authoring App (D18 decision 1, optional) -----------------------
# A second GitHub App, distinct from the Pullwright Approver above, that
# every authoring act — cloning a target repository, pushing a branch,
# opening or commenting on a pull request or issue — runs under when it is
# configured, in place of the GH_TOKEN PAT above (lib/author-token.sh,
# lib/forge-auth.sh). Never the same App as the Approver: one identity able
# to both author and approve its own work would recreate the self-approval
# D18 exists to retire.
#
# Leave all three empty and every cycle runs exactly as it always has: GH_TOKEN
# above authenticates every git/gh call, unchanged. Nothing bricks — this
# identity only ever adds a preferred credential on top of GH_TOKEN, never
# replaces it as a requirement, and `scripts/doctor.sh` reports which one a
# node is actually authoring with either way.
#
# The App id is not a secret. The installation id is the installation on
# *this* organisation: Settings → GitHub Apps → the App → Install App, or
# read it from `/orgs/<org>/installations`.
PULLWRIGHT_AUTHOR_APP_ID=
PULLWRIGHT_AUTHOR_INSTALLATION_ID=

# An absolute path **on the host** to the App's `.pem` private key. compose
# bind-mounts it read-only at this same path inside the container, the same
# fixed-path-both-sides shape the Approver's own key above uses. The file
# must be readable by uid 1000 (the container's `agent` user); `chmod 600`
# owned by a uid-1000 host user is the intended shape. Keep it out of every
# repository — it is the one credential here that cannot be rotated by
# editing a token field.
PULLWRIGHT_AUTHOR_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=

# --- Vercel (optional) ---------------------------------------------------
# poetic-fiddle deploys from GitHub through Vercel, so every pull request gets
# its own preview deployment — and nothing in the pull request's checks says
# whether that preview built or serves, because Vercel reports through GitHub's
# deployments API rather than as a check run. scripts/preview-deploy.sh is how
# a stage asks, and these are what it needs.
#
# Leave both empty and every cycle runs as before; the check then reports
# "could not check" instead of a verdict, which is not a failure.
#
# The bypass secret: Vercel → the project → Settings → Deployment Protection →
# Protection Bypass for Automation → generate. Preview deployments sit behind
# Vercel Authentication, and without this every one of them answers the login
# page — to a naive check, indistinguishable from a healthy deployment.
# Regenerating it in Vercel invalidates this copy on every node at once.
VERCEL_AUTOMATION_BYPASS_SECRET=

# Optional even when the above is set: a Vercel access token (Account Settings
# → Tokens), scoped to the team, with an expiry. It buys the tail of the build
# log when a deployment failed; without it a failure still names the
# deployment's inspector URL, which is where a human would go anyway.
VERCEL_TOKEN=

# --- Tailscale (tailnet profile) -----------------------------------------
# A pre-authentication key from the tailnet's admin console. Use a reusable,
# ephemeral-off, pre-approved key if you are provisioning several nodes; it is
# consumed once at first start and the node's identity then lives in the
# tailscale-state volume. Leave it set even after that first start: the
# sidecar's entrypoint refuses to start with it empty (compose.yaml), because
# a tailscaled that cannot authenticate registers a fresh node key on every
# restart instead of failing.
TS_AUTHKEY=

# --- Optional ------------------------------------------------------------
# The image to run. Override to test a locally built image (AGENT_OPS_IMAGE=agent-ops)
# or to pin a node to a known-good build by digest or SHA tag.
#AGENT_OPS_IMAGE=ghcr.io/poetic-poems/agent-ops:latest

# The container's timezone. Affects the cron schedule, so a node in another
# region still runs its cycles on the hours the operation expects.
#TZ=Pacific/Auckland

# The minute of each hour this node's implementation cycle fires (0-59,
# minus whatever config.json's schedule.excluded_minutes rules out — ours
# keeps 0 excluded because poetic's hourly sync workflow owns the top of
# the hour). Leave unset for the default: a stable hash of NODE_NAME spread
# over the allowed minutes, so several active nodes never all fire together
# on the one shared Claude account. The weekly review runs
# schedule.review_offset_minutes past CYCLE_MINUTE, mod 60, at
# schedule.review_hour. Set this when you want a minute you can remember, or
# when the fleet has outgrown an arbitrary spread — several nodes, or two
# nodes on one host, are better dealt out around the hour by hand. Changing
# it takes effect on the next `docker compose up -d`, which recreates the
# scheduler and re-renders the crontab: README.md, "Changing when a node's
# cycles run".
#CYCLE_MINUTE=

# The local profile's port on the host's loopback (the container always serves
# on 8787; this moves only the host side of the mapping). Set it when something
# else on this machine already holds 8787 — a second node's stack, say.
#DASHBOARD_PORT=8787

# The scheduler's network MTU. Set this to the host's egress MTU whenever that
# is below 1500 — behind WireGuard or Tailscale, on some cloud instances, and
# in WSL. Symptom of getting it wrong: DNS and plain HTTP work inside the
# container while every TLS connection hangs and resets, so `gh`, `claude` and
# `git clone` all fail as though the token were bad. Find it with:
#   ip route get 1.1.1.1        # names the egress interface
#   ip link show <interface>    # gives its mtu
#DOCKER_MTU=1500

# How often watchtower looks for a new image, in seconds. Five minutes is
# deliberately short: a roll that arrives while a cycle is running is deferred
# by the pre-update hook, and the poll interval is how long that deferral
# costs, so frequent polling is what makes waiting cheap.
#WATCHTOWER_POLL_INTERVAL=300

# A cron expression to roll on instead of polling — six fields, seconds first,
# in the container's TZ. Optional belt and braces to the pre-update hook: it
# puts the roll somewhere this node's cycles are not, rather than relying on
# the hook to notice one. Most nodes leave it unset.
#
# Set it and you must clear the poll interval in the same breath — watchtower
# refuses both at once ("Only schedule or interval can be defined, not both")
# and exits, which leaves this node silently not updating. So both lines,
# together, and the second one empty:
#
#   WATCHTOWER_SCHEDULE=0 40 * * * *
#   WATCHTOWER_POLL_INTERVAL=
#
# Pick a minute away from this node's own cycle minute — `docker compose logs
# scheduler | grep render-crontab` names it — and away from 03:00, when the
# weekly review runs.
#WATCHTOWER_SCHEDULE=

# The Docker Engine API version watchtower's (old) client advertises. The
# default 1.40 is accepted by every daemon this stack runs on; set this only if
# a particular host's daemon needs a different minimum. See compose.yaml.
#DOCKER_API_VERSION=1.40

# --- Resource ceilings ---------------------------------------------------
# Every container in the stack runs under a memory, CPU and process ceiling
# (D14). The defaults in compose.yaml were measured on a laptop and carry
# roughly 5x headroom over what the services were seen to use; a node on a
# real server should raise them rather than inherit a laptop's arithmetic.
#
# They are ceilings, not reservations — Docker reserves nothing, so the sum of
# them may exceed the host. On a host small enough that the sum is what binds
# (two nodes inside one WSL2 VM, say), add up the services this node's
# COMPOSE_PROFILES actually starts and keep the total under what the host can
# spare.
#
# The trade a memory ceiling makes: a stage that outgrows it is killed by the
# OOM killer mid-cycle rather than taking the host down. That is the intended
# exchange, but it is an exchange — a node losing cycles to exit 137 wants a
# larger number here, not a removed limit.
#AGENT_OPS_SCHEDULER_MEMORY=1536m
#AGENT_OPS_SCHEDULER_CPUS=2.0
#AGENT_OPS_DASHBOARD_MEMORY=512m
#AGENT_OPS_DASHBOARD_CPUS=1.0
#AGENT_OPS_TAILSCALE_MEMORY=256m
#AGENT_OPS_TAILSCALE_CPUS=0.5
#AGENT_OPS_WATCHTOWER_MEMORY=128m
#AGENT_OPS_WATCHTOWER_CPUS=0.5

# Process ceilings. A fork loop exhausts a host's pid space long before its
# memory, and takes the host with it rather than the container.
#AGENT_OPS_PIDS_LIMIT=1024
#AGENT_OPS_DASHBOARD_PIDS_LIMIT=512
#AGENT_OPS_SIDECAR_PIDS_LIMIT=256

# Container log rotation. Unbounded json-file logs are how a long-lived node
# fills a disk without anyone choosing to store anything.
#AGENT_OPS_LOG_MAX_SIZE=10m
#AGENT_OPS_LOG_MAX_FILE=5
